Do not report a failed setpriority() in a browser.

simc's sim_t defaults process_priority to BELOW_NORMAL (engine/sim/sim.cpp) and sim_t::execute()
calls computer_process::set_priority() on the main thread on every run. Under emscripten that
reaches the POSIX branch of engine/util/concurrency.cpp, because emscripten defines __unix__,
and emscripten's setpriority syscall stub returns -EPERM unconditionally — the nice value is
never even looked at. The perror() therefore fires on every single browser simulation:

    Could not set process priority.: Operation not permitted

It is harmless and completely uninformative. It cannot be avoided from the option side: this was
measured on the node relink of the locked build at process_priority=normal (nice 0), below_normal
(nice 5) and low (nice 10), and all three print the identical line.

This adds an #elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__) branch, placed BEFORE the SC_OSX/__unix__ one because
emscripten satisfies that condition, whose set_priority() does nothing and reports nothing —
exactly what the existing generic #else branch already does for platforms with no priority
concept. A browser tab has no process priority, so succeeding silently is the correct answer,
not a failure worth printing. Nothing outside emscripten builds is touched; every native
platform keeps the branch it compiles today.

Not sent upstream from here, and this project does not fork simc: it is a platform branch for a
target upstream does not build for. If upstream adds one, drop this file and bump
engine.lock.json.

NOT APPLIED TO ANY CURRENT ARTIFACT. Deferred to the next engine rebuild:
  - The shipped threaded simc.wasm was built before this existed. Its sha256 is referenced by
    public/engine/manifest.json, the deployment record and the release contract, and a crash-fix
    deploy is pending, so it is not being rebuilt for a log line.
  - Until it is, src/lib/simc/job.ts drops the line where engine stderr is classified, so the
    message does not reach the user-visible log or the notice classification. Delete that filter
    only once BOTH artifacts are rebuilt with this patch.
  - scripts/build-engine.sh applies patches/*.patch to the FALLBACK variant only; the threaded
    artifact builds straight from vendor/simc. Landing this in the shipped threaded binary needs
    that script to stage and patch the threaded variant too. Adding this file also changes the
    fallback staging stamp, so the next --fallback build re-stages and rebuilds from scratch.

diff --git a/engine/util/concurrency.cpp b/engine/util/concurrency.cpp
--- a/engine/util/concurrency.cpp
+++ b/engine/util/concurrency.cpp
@@ -255,6 +255,23 @@
  }
 }
 
+#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+// Emscripten defines __unix__, so without this branch it takes the POSIX one below and calls
+// setpriority(), which emscripten's syscall stub answers with -EPERM unconditionally
+// (system/lib/libc/emscripten_syscall_stubs.c: __syscall_setpriority returns -EPERM, whatever
+// the nice value). sim_t defaults process_priority to BELOW_NORMAL and sim_t::execute() calls
+// set_priority() on every run, so every browser sim prints
+//     Could not set process priority.: Operation not permitted
+// to stderr before it does anything. Nothing can be done about it from the option side:
+// process_priority=normal is nice 0 and fails identically.
+//
+// A browser tab has no process priority to set, so the correct behaviour here is the same as
+// the generic #else branch: do nothing, successfully.
+void computer_process::set_priority( priority_e )
+{
+  // No process priority in a browser sandbox. Not an error, so do not report one.
+}
+
 #elif defined(SC_OSX) || defined(__unix__)
 #include <sys/time.h>
 #include <sys/resource.h>
